• The sentence **"It has been found that the trees are being destroyed by a moth." **is **grammatically correct as it stands.**

• It correctly uses the present perfect continuous tense to indicate an action that started in the past and is still continuing.

• No substitutions are needed for the given sentence to be grammatically correct and clear.

Therefore, the correct answer is **Option 1: No substitution required**.



 [IMAGE: https://cdn.repeatermock.com/tb/f13e2ad6582c16aa62a83672e2547ad20a01fddb61962a1acce42eb9a1a99caa.png] **Additional Information__**


• **Option 2: "have destroyed"** – This changes the tense and meaning of the sentence, suggesting the destruction is already complete.

• **Option 3: "have been destroying"** – This implies the trees have been continuously destroyed up to now, but it is not as precise as the original sentence.

• **Option 4: "is being destroyed"** – This changes the subject-verb agreement, making the sentence incorrect.

• **No substitution required** – This is the correct option as the original sentence is already correct.
